es; whether these are separate individuals or suckers of one clump cannot be determined.","reference_ids":["reference-1"],"uncertain":true},{"id":"T19-ground-transition","priority":"variation","observation":"Ground contact is visible only in reference-0, where the trunk meets a grassy earth bank with a swollen, fibre-covered basal flare and no exposed stilt roots; no ground-level evidence exists in reference-1 or reference-2 despite the P-BASE view label.","reference_ids":["reference-0"],"uncertain":true},{"id":"T20-trunk-colour-range","priority":"variation","observation":"Trunk colour ranges from uniform grey-brown weathered tone (reference-0) to warm red-brown with pale grey bleached stub faces (reference-1, reference-2); directional sunlight in the latter two accounts for part of the contrast.","reference_ids":["reference-0","reference-1","reference-2"],"uncertain":false}],"observations":["Three references are treated as independent records; no assumption is made that they depict the same individual, site, or a sequence of change over time.","View coverage is uneven: only reference-0 shows the whole plant, crown silhouette, fruit, and ground contact. reference-1 and reference-2 are both mid-trunk/crown-underside views at different magnifications; neither includes the stem base, so base-specific morphology (root mass, offshoots/suckers, soil interface) is unresolved.","Recognition rests primarily on the combination of an unbranched stubbed trunk with diamond-patterned persistent leaf bases, absence of a crownshaft, and a full rosette of long arching pinnate grey-green fronds; fruit is diagnostic when present but is not required for recognition.","Lighting and time of day differ markedly (backlit dusk with heavy cloud in reference-0; bright midday sun with blue sky in reference-1 and reference-2), so apparent foliage and trunk colour differences are confounded with illumination and should not be read as morphological differences.","Fern growth on the trunk and the 